  {
   "xml_index": 4,
   "xml_name": "L11_004_Drawing A Point",
   "display_name": "Drawing A Point",
   "questiontext_html": "<p>Time to draw. <b>matplotlib</b> is Python's best-known plotting library. You draw on an <b>axes</b> object, usually called <code>ax</code>:</p>\n<pre>import matplotlib.pyplot as plt\nfig, ax = plt.subplots()          # a new picture (figure) with one axes\nax.set_aspect(&quot;equal&quot;)            # same scale on x and y\nax.scatter([1, 4], [2, 3], color=&quot;red&quot;)               # dots at (1, 2) and (4, 3)\nax.plot([0, 4], [0, 3], color=&quot;blue&quot;)                 # a line from (0, 0) to (4, 3)\nax.fill([0, 2, 1], [0, 0, 2], color=&quot;green&quot;, alpha=0.4)   # a filled shape (a triangle)</pre>\n<p>Each method takes a <b>list of x values</b> and a <b>list of y values</b>. <code>alpha</code> makes a colour see-through.</p>\n<p>We give every shape a method <code>draw(ax)</code> that draws the shape on the axes it is given. For a point, that is one dot:</p>\n<pre>ax.scatter([self.x], [self.y], color=self.color)</pre>\n<p><b>How it is tested:</b> the tests do not need a real picture. They pass a small <b>fake</b> axes object (<code>FakeAx</code>) that simply records the calls made on it, and print them. This is a common testing trick.</p>\n<p><b>Seeing your picture:</b> at the bottom of the answer box there is a commented-out block. Remove the <code>#</code> signs and press <b>Pre-check</b>: the picture appears under the test results. (The first time, loading matplotlib takes a few seconds.)</p>\n<p>We also add <code>points()</code>, which returns a list of the points that make up the shape; for a point, just <code>[self]</code>. It will be useful later.</p>\n<p><b>Your task:</b> add the methods <code>draw(ax)</code> and <code>points()</code> to <code>Point</code>, as described.</p>\n<p>For example, drawing <code>Point(1, 2, \"red\")</code> on the fake axes records <code>('scatter', [1], [2], 'red')</code>.</p>",
   "answerpreload": "class Point:\n    def __init__(self, x, y, color=\"black\"):\n        self.x = x\n        self.y = y\n        self.color = color\n\n    def __str__(self):\n        return \"(\" + str(self.x) + \", \" + str(self.y) + \")\"\n\n    def move(self, dx, dy):\n        self.x = self.x + dx\n        self.y = self.y + dy\n\n    def distance_to(self, other):\n        return round(((other.x - self.x) ** 2 + (other.y - self.y) ** 2) ** 0.5, 2)\n\n    def draw(self, ax):\n        # write your code here\n\n    def points(self):\n        # write your code here\n\n\n# ---- See your drawing (practice site only; not graded) ----\n# Remove the # in front of the lines below, then press Pre-check.\n# (Put the # back before Check if Check becomes slow.)\n# import matplotlib.pyplot as plt\n# fig, ax = plt.subplots()\n# ax.set_aspect('equal')\n# p = Point(1, 2, \"red\")\n# p.draw(ax)\n# Point(3, 1, \"blue\").draw(ax)\n",
   "mark": 1.0,
   "tests": [
    {
     "testcode": "class FakeAx:  # records drawing calls instead of drawing\n    def __init__(self): self.calls = []\n    def scatter(self, xs, ys, **kw): self.calls.append(('scatter', list(xs), list(ys), kw.get('color')))\n    def plot(self, xs, ys, **kw): self.calls.append(('plot', list(xs), list(ys), kw.get('color')))\n    def fill(self, xs, ys, **kw): self.calls.append(('fill', list(xs), list(ys), kw.get('color')))\nax = FakeAx()\nPoint(1, 2, \"red\").draw(ax)\nfor c in ax.calls:\n    print(c)",
     "stdin": "",
     "expected": "('scatter', [1], [2], 'red')",
     "extra": "",
     "show": true,
     "example": true,
     "in_precheck": true,
     "in_check": true
    },
    {
     "testcode": "class FakeAx:  # records drawing calls instead of drawing\n    def __init__(self): self.calls = []\n    def scatter(self, xs, ys, **kw): self.calls.append(('scatter', list(xs), list(ys), kw.get('color')))\n    def plot(self, xs, ys, **kw): self.calls.append(('plot', list(xs), list(ys), kw.get('color')))\n    def fill(self, xs, ys, **kw): self.calls.append(('fill', list(xs), list(ys), kw.get('color')))\nax = FakeAx()\np = Point(0, 5)\np.draw(ax)\np.move(1, 1)\np.draw(ax)\nfor c in ax.calls:\n    print(c)",
     "stdin": "",
     "expected": "('scatter', [0], [5], 'black')\n('scatter', [1], [6], 'black')",
     "extra": "",
     "show": true,
     "example": true,
     "in_precheck": false,
     "in_check": true
    },
    {
     "testcode": "p = Point(7, 8)\nprint(p.points()[0] is p, len(p.points()))",
     "stdin": "",
     "expected": "True 1",
     "extra": "",
     "show": true,
     "example": true,
     "in_precheck": false,
     "in_check": true
    },
    {
     "testcode": "class FakeAx:  # records drawing calls instead of drawing\n    def __init__(self): self.calls = []\n    def scatter(self, xs, ys, **kw): self.calls.append(('scatter', list(xs), list(ys), kw.get('color')))\n    def plot(self, xs, ys, **kw): self.calls.append(('plot', list(xs), list(ys), kw.get('color')))\n    def fill(self, xs, ys, **kw): self.calls.append(('fill', list(xs), list(ys), kw.get('color')))\nax = FakeAx()\nPoint(-3, 4, \"green\").draw(ax)\nfor c in ax.calls:\n    print(c)",
     "stdin": "",
     "expected": "('scatter', [-3], [4], 'green')",
     "extra": "",
     "show": false,
     "example": false,
     "in_precheck": false,
     "in_check": true
    }
   ]
