  {
   "xml_index": 5,
   "xml_name": "L11_005_A Line Made Of Points",
   "display_name": "A Line Made Of Points",
   "questiontext_html": "<p>A <code>Line</code> is defined by its two end <b>points</b>, so its attributes are two <code>Point</code> objects. An object made of other objects is called <b>composition</b>. The line can then use the points' own methods, for example <code>self.p1.distance_to(self.p2)</code> for its length.</p>\n<div style=\"margin:6px 0 4px\"><div style=\"display:inline-block;vertical-align:top;border:1px solid #2a8c8c;border-radius:10px;margin:4px 10px 4px 0;min-width:120px;background:#eef7f7\"><div style=\"padding:3px 10px;font-family:monospace;font-size:13px;color:#1d6464;border-bottom:1px solid #b9dcdc\">line : Line</div><div style=\"padding:4px 10px\"><div style=\"font-family:monospace;font-size:13px\">p1 = &#x27;Point(0, 0)&#x27;</div><div style=\"font-family:monospace;font-size:13px\">p2 = &#x27;Point(3, 4)&#x27;</div><div style=\"font-family:monospace;font-size:13px\">color = &#x27;blue&#x27;</div></div></div><div style=\"display:inline-block;padding:22px 8px 0 0;color:#8a939c\">&rarr;</div><div style=\"display:inline-block;vertical-align:top;border:1px solid #2a8c8c;border-radius:10px;margin:4px 10px 4px 0;min-width:120px;background:#eef7f7\"><div style=\"padding:3px 10px;font-family:monospace;font-size:13px;color:#1d6464;border-bottom:1px solid #b9dcdc\">p1 : Point</div><div style=\"padding:4px 10px\"><div style=\"font-family:monospace;font-size:13px\">x = 0</div><div style=\"font-family:monospace;font-size:13px\">y = 0</div></div></div><div style=\"display:inline-block;vertical-align:top;border:1px solid #2a8c8c;border-radius:10px;margin:4px 10px 4px 0;min-width:120px;background:#eef7f7\"><div style=\"padding:3px 10px;font-family:monospace;font-size:13px;color:#1d6464;border-bottom:1px solid #b9dcdc\">p2 : Point</div><div style=\"padding:4px 10px\"><div style=\"font-family:monospace;font-size:13px\">x = 3</div><div style=\"font-family:monospace;font-size:13px\">y = 4</div></div></div></div>\n<p><b>Indentation reminder:</b> the body of an <code>if</code>, loop, function or class must be indented (press Tab or type 4 spaces). Only the spaces at the start of a line matter.</p>\n<p><b>Your task:</b> the full <code>Point</code> class is given. Write the class <code>Line</code> whose constructor takes two points <code>p1</code>, <code>p2</code> and an optional <code>color</code> (default <code>\"black\"</code>), with:</p>\n<ul><li><code>length()</code> returning the distance between the two points (rounded to 2 decimal places, as <code>distance_to</code> already does);</li><li><code>__str__</code> returning the two points joined by <code>-</code>, for example <code>(0, 0)-(3, 4)</code>.</li></ul>\n<p>For example, <code>Line(Point(0, 0), Point(3, 4)).length()</code> returns <code>5.0</code>.</p>",
   "answerpreload": "class Point:\n    def __init__(self, x, y, color=\"black\"):\n        self.x = x\n        self.y = y\n        self.color = color\n\n    def __str__(self):\n        return \"(\" + str(self.x) + \", \" + str(self.y) + \")\"\n\n    def move(self, dx, dy):\n        self.x = self.x + dx\n        self.y = self.y + dy\n\n    def distance_to(self, other):\n        return round(((other.x - self.x) ** 2 + (other.y - self.y) ** 2) ** 0.5, 2)\n\n    def draw(self, ax):\n        ax.scatter([self.x], [self.y], color=self.color)\n\n    def points(self):\n        return [self]\n\n\nclass Line:\n    def __init__(self, p1, p2, color=\"black\"):\n        # write your code here\n\n    def length(self):\n        # write your code here\n\n    def __str__(self):\n        # write your code here\n",
   "mark": 1.0,
   "tests": [
